diff --git a/docs/native-monitor.md b/docs/native-monitor.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..8422156e9c79c7e05ffacc8e9ff3ad0fa9ad884d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/native-monitor.md
@@ -0,0 +1,129 @@
+# Native Monitor Protocol extension
+
+This development branch adds a Monitor Protocol v0 consumer to Codex App Server.
+The consumer is a Rust/Tokio task owned by the runtime. The private journal
+currently supports Unix platforms (macOS and Linux); this preview was built on macOS arm64. It does not execute a
+polling shell, run `codex exec`, or create another conversation.
+
+## Scope and ownership
+
+- A person opens a Codex frontend and authorizes monitoring in that conversation.
+- `monitor_start` binds the existing thread to its single subscribed frontend
+  connection. Zero or multiple subscribers make the initial binding ambiguous and
+  are refused. A second subscriber never becomes a replacement owner.
+- An idle event enters the existing thread through automatic `start_turn_if_idle`
+  admission. During a running turn, the observation stays pending until idle.
+  The notification is a named function output, not a new user instruction.
+- The worker survives completed turns. Disconnect, unsubscribe, thread shutdown,
+  user interruption, or `monitor_stop` cancels it. A loaded thread or a live daemon PID alone does not
+  establish ownership. There is no automatic reattachment after runtime restart.
+- Plan mode and runtime shutdown admission still apply. Pending observations are
+  retained when admission is refused.
+
+Revocation prevents a queued wake that has not yet passed Core admission. A turn
+already admitted, or an acknowledgement already received by the remote server,
+can finish; cancellation does not roll back either.
+
+The connection is the runtime's attendance boundary; it cannot prove that a
+person is physically looking at the terminal. Closing the owning frontend is the
+mechanical lifetime boundary.
+
+## Register a source
+
+The operator creates an existing, participant-specific subscription on an MP v0
+server. Before starting the rebuilt App Server, place a source registry at
+`$CODEX_HOME/native-monitors/sources.json` (normally under `~/.codex`):
+
+```json
+{
+  "constructo": {
+    "base_url": "http://127.0.0.1:PORT",
+    "subscription": "EXISTING_SUBSCRIPTION_ID",
+    "token_env": "MENTU_MONITOR_READER_TOKEN"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Supply that subscription's reader token in the named environment variable of
+the rebuilt App Server. Do not put a bearer in a prompt or tool arguments. The
+registry is snapshotted at server startup. The model can choose a registered
+source name; it cannot pair an arbitrary environment secret with a destination.
+Remote sources require HTTPS. HTTP is allowed only on loopback. Redirects are
+refused. The source must implement MP v0 pull and acknowledge endpoints; a legacy
+Construct join page alone is not that endpoint.
+
+## Native tools
+
+| Tool | Purpose |
+| --- | --- |
+| `monitor_start({"source":"constructo"})` | Arm the configured subscription for this attended thread. |
+| `monitor_status({})` | Inspect native worker state and pending delivery. |
+| `monitor_show({"monitor_id":"…","delivery":"…"})` | Inspect the stored observation as untrusted external data. |
+| `monitor_handled({"monitor_id":"…","delivery":"…","evidence":"…"})` | Persist the actual disposition or its record reference. |
+| `monitor_stop({"monitor_id":"…"})` | Cancel and join the worker, preserving unacknowledged data. |
+
+Source journals use one OS lock per subscription under the configured Codex home.
+After a crash, an explicit start in the same persistent thread can recover the
+journal under its newly attended owner. Durable receipts retry acknowledgement
+without delivering the event again; unhandled events keep their delivery identity
+and receive fresh activation metadata. A different thread cannot adopt the journal.
+Validated empty filtered ranges use a separate durable scan receipt and advance
+without waking the model; they cannot skip a pending observation. No recovery starts itself. Session history is bounded to 64 monitor activations.
+Fatal source or journal failures are also reported to the owning frontend as a
+native warning, without starting a model turn.
+
+An observation carries delivery identity and fingerprint, with instructions to
+inspect it under existing authority. Receipt and source acknowledgement are
+separate steps. Enqueueing, printing, or inspecting does not acknowledge handling.
+The local receipt is an assertion of handling, not verification that an external
+action succeeded. For Construct work, record the disposition as the authenticated
+actor and pass that record reference as evidence.
+
+## Build and verify
+
+Pinned starting point: upstream `openai/codex`
+`4aa94dce270de668eff6e2fa8585c82385e84455`.
+
+From `codex-rs`, with the repository's Rust toolchain installed:
+
+```sh
+CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG=0 CARGO_PROFILE_TEST_DEBUG=0 CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0 \
+  cargo test -p codex-monitor-extension --lib
+
+CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG=0 CARGO_PROFILE_TEST_DEBUG=0 CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0 \
+  cargo test -p codex-app-server --test all v2::native_monitor
+
+CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG=0 CARGO_PROFILE_TEST_DEBUG=0 CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0 \
+  cargo test -p codex-core --lib start_guard
+
+CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG=0 CARGO_PROFILE_TEST_DEBUG=0 CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0 \
+  cargo test -p codex-app-server --lib native_monitor::tests
+
+CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG=0 CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0 cargo build -p codex-cli --bin codex
+```
+
+Integration tests use local MP and canned Responses servers. They do not call a
+real model or consume a user's provider credentials. The native test must observe
+an event published after the preceding turn completed, a new turn on the same
+thread, no acknowledgement before disposition, and no wake after unsubscribe
+while the thread remains loaded.
+
+Run `target/debug/codex --no-daemon` from a human-opened terminal to use the
+rebuilt runtime instead of reconnecting to the installed shared daemon. Do not
+overwrite the binary or daemon serving a current conversation. An installed
+Codex release cannot load this Rust extension through a skill, plugin, hook, or
+MCP configuration; the rebuilt runtime must actually own the new session. A TUI
+that reconnects to an older daemon will continue using that older runtime.
+
